      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Vector Equation of the Plane Perpendicular to a Given Vector",
      "problem": "Find an equation of the plane that contains the point $Q(3,2,2)$ and is perpendicular to the vector $n=⟨2,2,3⟩.$",
      "worked_solution": "First, notice that the position vector of $Q$ is $q=⟨3,2,2⟩,$ $n=⟨2,2,3⟩$ is normal to the plane.\n\nThe equation of the plane that passes though the point $Q({x}_{0},{y}_{0},{z}_{0})$ and is perpendicular to the vector $n$ can be given as\n\n$$\n(r-q)\\cdot n=0,\n$$\n\nwhere $r$ is the position vector of any point on the plane, and $q$ is the position vector of $Q.$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-q)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-q\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =q\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨2,2,3⟩ & =⟨3,2,2⟩\\cdot ⟨2,2,3⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨2,2,3⟩ & =16.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
