    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-127911",
      "knowledge_point_id": "b0d93156-f9be-5e6e-976a-bef30dc892ee",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding a Vector That Is Parallel To a Plane",
      "problem": "A nonzero vector $⟨a,\\,0,\\,c⟩$ is parallel to the plane $r\\cdot ⟨-1,2,2⟩=\\sqrt{11}.$ What is the value of $\\frac{a}{c}?$",
      "worked_solution": "We need a vector $v=⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩$ that is parallel to the given plane. \n\nFrom the equation $r\\cdot ⟨-1,2,2⟩=\\sqrt{11}$ of the plane, we get the normal vector $n=⟨-1,2,2⟩.$ So, $v⊥n$ and we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ ⟨{t}_{1},{t}_{2},{t}_{3}⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,2,2⟩ & =0 \\\\ -{t}_{1}+2{t}_{2}+2{t}_{3} & =0 \\\\ {t}_{1} & =2{t}_{2}+2{t}_{3}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence, we obtain \n\n$$\nv=⟨2{t}_{2}+2{t}_{3},\\,{t}_{2},\\,{t}_{3}⟩,\n$$\n\nwhere ${t}_{2},{t}_{3}\\in (-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ \n\nWe are told that the second component of $v$ equals $0.$ So, we must set ${t}_{2}=0.$ \n\nSince $v$ is nonzero, we can pick any ${t}_{3}\\ne 0.$ For example, setting ${t}_{3}=1$, we get\n\n$$\nv=⟨2,\\,0,\\,1⟩.\n$$\n\nTherefore, $\\frac{a}{c}=2.$",
      "answer_fields": [
        {
          "key": "selection",
          "type": "radio",
