    {
      "math_academy_id": "q-70892",
      "knowledge_point_id": "45cdbb0d-2986-5c05-8d23-dce29363051b",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Vector Equation of the Plane Perpendicular to a Given Straight Line",
      "problem": "Find an equation of the plane that passes through the point $Q(2,1,-1)$ and is perpendicular to the line that goes through the points \n$A(2,1,0)$ and $B(3,2,1).$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we note that the position vector of $Q$ is $q=⟨2,1,-1⟩.$\n\nSince the line  passes through the points $A(2,1,0)$ and $B(3,2,1)$ we can find a vector parallel to the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨3,2,1⟩-⟨2,1,0⟩ \\\\  & =⟨1,1,1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, a normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨1,1,1⟩.$ \n\nTherefore, the equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-q)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-q\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =q\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨1,1,1⟩ & =⟨2,1,-1⟩\\cdot ⟨1,1,1⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨1,1,1⟩ & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
