      "math_academy_id": "q-127948",
      "knowledge_point_id": "45cdbb0d-2986-5c05-8d23-dce29363051b",
      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Vector Equation of the Plane Perpendicular to a Given Straight Line",
      "problem": "Find an equation of the plane that passes through the point $Q(-3,2,1)$ and is  perpendicular to the line \n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-1+6t \\\\ y=3-5t \\\\ z=-2+4t.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "worked_solution": "Note that the position vector of $Q$ is $q=⟨-3,2,1⟩.$ Converting the parametric equations of the line to vector form, we get\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}x=-1+6t \\\\ y=3-5t \\\\ z=-2+4t\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,⟨x,y,z⟩=⟨-1,3,-2⟩+t\\text{ }{{⟨6,-5,4⟩}_{⏟}}_{v}.\n$$\n\n   \n\nThe direction vector of the line is $v=⟨6,-5,4⟩.$ So, a normal  vector to the plane is \n\n$$\nn=v=⟨6,-5,4⟩.\n$$\n\n \n\nTherefore, the equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-q)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-q\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =q\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨6,-5,4⟩ & =⟨-3,2,1⟩\\cdot ⟨6,-5,4⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨6,-5,4⟩ & =-24.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
