      "knowledge_point": "Finding the Vector Equation of the Plane Perpendicular to a Given Straight Line",
      "problem": "Find an equation of the plane that passes through the point $Q(1,-4,2)$ and is perpendicular to the line that goes through the points \n$A(2,1,-3)$ and $B(1,6,-4).$",
      "worked_solution": "First, we note that the position vector of $Q$ is $q=⟨1,-4,2⟩.$\n\nSince the line  passes through the points $A(2,1,-3)$ and $B(1,6,-4)$ we can find a vector parallel to the line:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{\\to }{AB} & =⟨1,6,-4⟩-⟨2,1,-3⟩ \\\\  & =⟨-1,5,-1⟩.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nSo, a normal vector to the plane is $n=⟨-1,5,-1⟩.$\n\nTherefore, the equation of the plane is given by\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(r-q)\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n-q\\cdot n & =0 \\\\ r\\cdot n & =q\\cdot n \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-1,5,-1⟩ & =⟨1,-4,2⟩\\cdot ⟨-1,5,-1⟩ \\\\ r\\cdot ⟨-1,5,-1⟩ & =-23.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
